{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794660127,"nonce":"z3byIhD9KR1rJJxVeeMtd3tcvvU=","message":"Exporter performance, committed locally as df3ae93e (not pushed). Profiled with per-call timers on the Haxe unit suite (3501 modules, 334k nodes), a stock compile (typing and filters, no exporter) being about 2.5 s on a quiet machine.\n\nWhere the first version's time went (macro side, about 3.2 s plus 0.24 s to write):\n- f.expr(): 0.9-1.1 s over 10,033 calls. This is the compiler converting each function body into macro values (about 3.7 us a node). It is inherent to the macro API and is not removed.\n- type interning per node: 867 ms over 291k calls. Context.follow cost 7.4 us on each of 39.8k function-type nodes and was not needed; Ref.get() on an anonymous structure cost 38.7 us on each of 13.4k nodes (517 ms) although only 429 distinct structures exist.\n- the rest of the expression walk: about 0.9 s, much of it eight array pushes per node and the second pass that wrote them out.\n- assemble: 224 ms.\n\nWhat changed:\n- Types are found by the cheapest key their shape allows: a class, abstract or enum without arguments by its path, a typedef by its name, anything else by the compiler's own text for the type (TypeTools.toString, 2.6-3 us), and the structure is read only on a miss. Context.follow is gone from the hot path (it also stripped Null\u003cT\u003e).\n- Node records are written straight into a buffer that doubles, with no per-node arrays and no assemble loop.\n- A node's children are queued as they are met. findField no longer fills a class mid-walk, which is what forced the double buffer.\n- The generated Haxe writer packs sub-word fields that fill a word into one write (kind, flags and padding are one setInt32).\n- LocalRec lost haxe_id: the compiler's variable counter shifted whenever the exporter's own source changed (typing the macro allocates variables), so output differed between exporter versions for no reason. Output is now reproducible across versions; the only difference between two runs of the unit suite is the \"Generated at\" string the tests build in.\n\nResult: type interning 867 -\u003e about 280 ms, assemble 224 -\u003e 40-70 ms, expression stage 1.8 -\u003e 1.3 s. On a machine under load (load average 25) the median CPU time added over a stock compile fell from 4.07 s to 2.91 s (5 interleaved runs each; stock 2.67 s CPU). What remains is the 1 s of f.expr() and the interpreter's per-operation cost.\n\nScale: Ashui's smoke test (215k nodes, 6.9 MB) exports in 1.9 s and its components test (259k nodes, 8.3 MB) in 2.3 s on top of a 3.3-3.8 s typecheck; the unit suite (334k nodes) takes 2.6-2.9 s. Cost per node is flat across the three, so it grows linearly. Rust opens and checks each file in 2-3 ms.\n\nKnown limits: type parameters with the same name and structure share an id (the cache key is the compiler's text), and two typedefs with identical fields get separate ids.","files":null}]}